<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arganthonios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 670–c. 550 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Arganthonius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arganthonius was a king of Tartessos in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who says he reigned for 80 years and lived to 120. He welcomed the Phocaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> who traded on his coasts and offered them land to settle; when they declined, he paid for a wall around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. He is the only Tartessian king known by name from reliable sources.</p></div>
